aviente/tests/reports/

# Family recipe content — kept out of the repo while it is PUBLIC.
# The seed is regenerable from these local files:
#   node tools/seed-from-source.mjs --commit
# Remove this block once the repo is private and you want the seed versioned.
my_recipes*.json
my_recipes*.md
aviente/supabase/seed.sql

# Generated import payloads — regenerate with tools/parse-markdown-book.mjs
metakonim-import.json
aviente/repairs.json
aviente/recategorise.json
aviente/repairs-categories.json

# ── private family data — the repo is a template; the data lives behind auth ──
# The recipes, the photos and the kids starter are the family's content, not the
# app's. They were tracked and pushed while the repo was public; removing them here
# stops NEW commits carrying them, but the pushed history still contains every one
# until the repo goes private. Cache-removal is not retraction.
מתכונים.md
kids-starter.json
photos/*.jpg
photos/*.jpeg
photos/*.png
my_recipes_gemini.json
aviente/public/_upload/

# ── backups and diagnostics — never versioned ────────────────────────────────
# `aviente-backup-<date>.json` is the WHOLE cookbook in one file: 74 recipes, 721
# ingredients, 420 steps. One got committed and pushed to this repo while it was
# public, because `git add -A` was run from `aviente/` while the git root is one level
# up — so `-A` staged every untracked file at the root as well. The patterns are
# deliberately wide: a backup is named for the day it was taken, so an exact filename
# can never cover the next one.
aviente-backup-*.json
# tools/backup-check.mjs writes here. Anchored: a bare `backups/` would also match any
# future app/api/.../backups route, the way `photos/` once un-shipped one.
/aviente/backups/
supabase_logs*.json
*.dc.html
# Design references dropped in the root while working. The one that got committed was
# 432KB of somebody else's menu.
menu sample*.jpg
